#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119541 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "c7f85c6d-a7d6-5ae6-b82b-7e8608b6411e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21757" :question/problem "![](images/27/270055d134c778004d3e31c76b700bd57c62f1cde2cd103356fef63b2be3ff9e.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t = 0\\,\\textrm s$ is $1\\,\\textrm{A}.$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $12\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 12\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 12I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 2\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 2\\cdot\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4 = 12I + 2\\cdot\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 6I = 2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 6I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $I_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + 6\\alpha = 2\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $I_p(t) = \\dfrac13.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-6t} + \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\textrm{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0) = 1,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-6(0)}+\\frac{1}{3} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{e}^{-6t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c = \\dfrac23e^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The constant $I_p = \\dfrac13$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to\\infty,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119542 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/id #uuid "9be480e2-68b0-591c-b331-0751a225122c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21758" :question/problem "![](images/e3/e3ab0152f1898f25b6fbf431ba398be01af8f9dad6664f27c28ea7549bff18e5.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $E(t) = 7\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $14\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $7\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 14\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 14I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 7\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 7\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nThe source voltage (EMF) is given by\n\n$$\nE(t) = 7\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n7\\sin(3t) = 14I + 7\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n7\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 7\\sin(3t) - 14I,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\sin(3t) - 2I,\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2I = \\sin(3t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "2594ae82-9bd8-5ee4-a5a9-4be27b5d9097" :question/math-academy-id "e-21761" :question/problem "![](images/67/6729737d837bef39687106d530899d797dd69c54c92c2d31c2afd99405bc03e5.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, **rounded to two decimal places.**" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 5\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 5I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5\\sin(3t) = 5I + \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I = 5\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(3t) + \\beta\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(5\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +5\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{15}{34},$ $\\beta = \\dfrac{25}{34}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = -\\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) = - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{15}{34}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{25}{34}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.86\\,\\textrm{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aedf5696-b202-5101-a939-41ba9ccd7759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aedf5696-b202-5101-a939-41ba9ccd7759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441917} :answer-field/id #uuid "3938876a-481c-59e0-9eb4-db77374428b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aba18e21-a0ae-54e4-91a4-0824d0fc96eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-336215" :question/problem "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $20\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $2\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.01$ seconds given that the initial current is $2\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.01$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5=20I+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+10{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+10\\alpha =\\frac{5}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{1}{4} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(7{e}^{-10t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.01$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.01)=\\frac{1}{4}(7{e}^{-10(0.01)}+1)≈1.83\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15765ceb-c21b-5d75-8bab-59640665051b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-10I=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441901} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe27691-a46e-52d0-ab71-166079f3d08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-40I=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441908} {:answer/id #uuid "7814a7c6-4a23-5616-8c4b-d78da059e449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+40I=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441913} {:answer/id #uuid "8f65e13e-8ff1-5838-8e62-92f884f5c545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441915} {:answer/id #uuid "d31d4135-c6f8-5435-93db-b00252a59a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f65e13e-8ff1-5838-8e62-92f884f5c545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441915} :answer-field/id #uuid "35285a5f-8b43-594b-bed5-a8b1f342b566" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e14b31da-25df-5fa5-9bfc-4a5fa3c83d9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336528" :question/problem "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds)." :question/worked-solution "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=40I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "274a2cc1-c6d0-5962-822d-9a8c9ddaa5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309042} {:answer/id #uuid "2e48893f-9fba-5c4c-ae0b-4fc497bb2b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-12I=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309097} {:answer/id #uuid "997e601f-0f2a-5497-9671-86a0d5ad8488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-36I=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309985} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ef3b11-a1e3-5477-8c06-266af01a618c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310203} {:answer/id #uuid "e19621c8-586a-575f-a508-a9642143c0ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+36I=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "274a2cc1-c6d0-5962-822d-9a8c9ddaa5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309042} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9391c76-d036-5508-af53-b044eb031910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcff7887-b892-5287-9b6f-79b17b67af6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336534" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c644931a5d7c3b015e00518269920718cde39882b45d3589e12e6b90d0391d46.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $36\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=36\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=36I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n9=36I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b64b87f-eca7-5f41-834e-0c109a362f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-6I=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441907} {:answer/id #uuid "6c963e0a-5b77-567a-8321-54badb205db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-4I=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441911} {:answer/id #uuid "996409b0-e6fa-57e7-99b9-cd8b61cd400f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441916} {:answer/id #uuid "afc3c83d-c96b-579a-8e82-93fce2051362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441918} {:answer/id #uuid "f76c4adb-4da0-59aa-acf5-290d24892ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "996409b0-e6fa-57e7-99b9-cd8b61cd400f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441916} :answer-field/id #uuid "fec331a1-3371-57b3-ae6b-a96dcb6d5ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d642175b-da68-5c1f-a969-cd59bcc47e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336544" :question/problem "![](images/da/da02d808aac69b1b7381f6e6ca7a006b863e6b808a89ecc7a4f79be0db7556b9.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $24\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6=24I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0df37092-9abd-5315-bda3-c22df3f4d3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308842} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac928da-4aae-5791-9178-94905f8f898b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308958} {:answer/id #uuid "316e9b21-7c74-5daa-99c8-41c72ce2c8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-9I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309121} {:answer/id #uuid "7061b022-b644-5447-a74c-fab3d94b05ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309619} {:answer/id #uuid "876f04be-d390-511b-94d1-fb8c95d435f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0df37092-9abd-5315-bda3-c22df3f4d3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308842} :answer-field/id #uuid "d836e45f-1d6a-552f-a43c-42243659bd7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b05808bc-cc2d-5a25-af93-5fe5382438db" :question/math-academy-id "q-336546" :question/problem "![](images/99/999acaa0bc014b7afc7c916dd03c957e15707ca680836341e5a071a1f321e97d.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $E(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $9\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=9\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=9I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=9I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6905d1fa-aede-5dec-8b9c-01fc827b99e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441909} {:answer/id #uuid "6f549ead-c18c-53dc-9b35-4c7cf5360aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441912} {:answer/id #uuid "c3085092-7447-53c2-ab73-8ce83fc8ca20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441920} {:answer/id #uuid "c66c7a80-1279-5afd-8698-02299e425280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-10I=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441921} {:answer/id #uuid "e82fada6-004d-5f78-9c37-7f317f030764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-5I=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6905d1fa-aede-5dec-8b9c-01fc827b99e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441909} :answer-field/id #uuid "be5ee839-525c-52d1-82fe-55826f7f18d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d671cb3b-5175-53db-90fa-e1b9c7534825" :question/math-academy-id "q-336549" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a84a1501854f6efb988b7c02c3a1b5a80b80dbf3d5fc7ce94406778cc6dcadb5.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $E(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $10\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=10I+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b085f7b4-1f0e-56f8-a51f-0da8860bf7ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377221} {:answer/id #uuid "ed100424-66cf-5f20-9b03-93d588806dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed100424-66cf-5f20-9b03-93d588806dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441924} :answer-field/id #uuid "8749cc96-0ec2-5518-bf4f-7c4b6f7a3aeb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "baddbe2e-5062-58fc-a5b7-9bf88f202c4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336642" :question/problem "The current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=6,\\,I(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state current for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state current is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{3}{4}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $\\frac{3}{4}$ amperes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ad8d9fe-57f9-5604-afd9-cca8857d8b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.625" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309709} {:answer/id #uuid "35ebd7fe-0ae4-53ef-a9ea-c17e7e684c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35ebd7fe-0ae4-53ef-a9ea-c17e7e684c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441905} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd076ae9-38f5-5156-8a82-4b96278c1336" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b7d51f8-bb26-591f-afab-21ba5979983e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336645" :question/problem "The current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=5,\\,I(0)=3.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state current for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state current is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =5\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{5}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{8}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{5}{8}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $\\frac{5}{8}$ amperes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f27e85-ca05-5649-bf8e-533410517cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4{e}^{-10t}+1)/5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377224} {:answer/id #uuid "201094b7-9ea7-547e-8ae2-50c5f5152016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}e^{-10t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "201094b7-9ea7-547e-8ae2-50c5f5152016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}e^{-10t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441903} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9777acc-bd37-5aea-a411-5ae6180e338a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ae77f4e-88f7-5352-a9db-21c8f98ada40" :question/math-academy-id "q-336646" :question/problem "![](images/25/256c09422d242886f67b02abba2d2e8887b4dea1f45332dc57a106aabc2154f9.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $20\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4=20I+2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+10{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+10\\alpha =2\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{1}{5} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4{e}^{-10t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-10t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{5}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eaffa99-cc6c-5d54-b37b-23bfe3c8a85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2eaffa99-cc6c-5d54-b37b-23bfe3c8a85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309104} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b08292d-e962-5f52-bc98-0912eae58e8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f82f1e2-e41a-5b2b-b124-a17d0fcfb4a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-336653" :question/problem "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $16\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $2\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.03$ seconds given that the initial current is $3\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.03$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4=16I+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =2\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $3\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =3 \\\\ A{e}^{-8(0)}+\\frac{1}{4} & =3 \\\\ A & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(11{e}^{-8t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.03$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.03)=\\frac{1}{4}(11{e}^{-8(0.03)}+1)≈2.41\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25bc7e77-a1ba-503a-9c91-af56d4db8aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441904} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4bad54-9152-542d-909c-398b06293aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441906} {:answer/id #uuid "69b63830-b726-5d99-92b7-09497a69031c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441910} {:answer/id #uuid "870c4e55-f399-5e97-a85e-6ca59c9165d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441914} {:answer/id #uuid "bdca223a-55f2-5ca4-98ab-6d1397c5cd69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "870c4e55-f399-5e97-a85e-6ca59c9165d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441914} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eefd91d-419f-55e9-93a6-15c6bdbecaa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b46ee022-1c34-5022-81e0-9eb66a5f128b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336658" :question/problem "![](images/e3/e329b7c1fdb9652affd6381c5002efe4c8eed6fa2b13da43c9a2a6116daa386b.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=5\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=5I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=5I+1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+5{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{12}{29},$ $\\beta =\\frac{30}{29}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{12}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{30}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{12}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{30}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{12}{29},\\,\\beta =\\frac{30}{29}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{12}{29}+\\frac{30}{29} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "113abfca-44ac-5d6e-a874-ba1f3189bcb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308868} {:answer/id #uuid "2699833b-ed8d-5b65-8a61-083ca31c9f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309036} {:answer/id #uuid "6a98fda5-9a32-5925-a251-336e837c2bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309578} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc00148-d552-5100-965f-48cbf7d75a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310007} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd44d9a-5708-5c64-be2d-8eb8f43474a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdd44d9a-5708-5c64-be2d-8eb8f43474a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310282} :answer-field/id #uuid "82376110-ca2a-5b6d-8980-c57f2af37109" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cc14ed2-ac96-5101-a8c2-632aca90e68e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336742" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e08922ed1028b9fa5b799d52d25aa6b5e23e71cfa1cbd4f152ac592422d68f8d.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=12I+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(8\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +8\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{8}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{2}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{17},\\,\\beta =\\frac{8}{17}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{2}{17}+\\frac{8}{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e02ad9-f01e-5d91-b67e-c8ac9b2f9690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32e02ad9-f01e-5d91-b67e-c8ac9b2f9690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377272} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7d1f7d0-ad3b-599f-aa11-6740016d6811" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcce442e-f4af-544c-929a-c36fe279cfc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-336815" :question/problem "![](images/97/97d661baf511c1e99ee8eed7fbed2ad02d9f96c452ece5b765a3e2cf657c431b.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=6I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(6\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +6\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 6\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{3},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{3}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}≈1.49\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17fb0566-042b-5e63-b5fb-b9534f0e3280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17fb0566-042b-5e63-b5fb-b9534f0e3280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441902} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c3b11f6-8e7a-5eeb-8b4a-ea3e87e2cec9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4a1842a-2984-5d5e-9da6-07b14124ecae" :question/math-academy-id "q-336816" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cc4feb2e391a0a25bb531592205dbc738f0fd0582fb2d56a1627a8584a42618.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=8I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=8I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(8\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +8\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{12}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{17})}^{2}+{(\\frac{12}{17})}^{2}}≈0.73\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56be3e3d-cc68-5b73-870d-17bf13a0d689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56be3e3d-cc68-5b73-870d-17bf13a0d689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377275} :answer-field/id #uuid "207cd688-1b68-5ccb-98fe-558f7b0217d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c771bb1c-fa6d-50e6-96f6-b48fda5177fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-336817" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b7ddd9e65fdbd1c8756c4cbf5690640736fb91c5d93bdba941ba62decb0bbf4.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $4\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=4\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=4I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=4I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(8\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +8\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{16}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{4}{17})}^{2}+{(\\frac{16}{17})}^{2}}≈0.97\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}]}
